-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/7707291b-476e-4542-9d37-dbff4c5087c4
-- title:
--   The flocking game on a finite graph: Markovian controls $\mathcal A_G$, state solutions of (2.1), costs (2.2)–(2.3), Markovian $\varepsilon$-Nash equilibrium (Definition 2.2)
-- statement:
--   Fix a finite simple graph $G$ on $V=\{1,\dots,n\}$, a horizon $T>0$, a noise level $\sigma>0$ and a constant $c>0$; the state space is $\mathbb R^V$ with its Euclidean norm $|\cdot|$.
--
--   1. **Admissible Markovian controls** $\mathcal A_G$: Borel functions $\alpha:[0,T]\times\mathbb R^V\to\mathbb R$ with $\sup_{(t,x)\in[0,T]\times\mathbb R^V} |\alpha(t,x)|/(1+|x|)<\infty$.
--   2. **State equation** (2.1): given a profile $(\alpha_v)_{v\in V}\in\mathcal A_G^V$ and a non-random initial state $x_0$,
--   $$dX_v(t) = \alpha_v(t,X(t))\,dt + \sigma\,dW_v(t),\qquad t\in[0,T],\quad X(0)=x_0,$$
--   with $(W_v)_v$ independent standard Brownian motions. A solution here is a continuous process, adapted to the completed filtration of $W$, satisfying almost surely $X_v(t)=x_{0,v}+\int_0^t\alpha_v(s,X(s))\,ds+\sigma W_v(t)$ for all $t\in[0,T]$.
--   3. **Cost** (2.2)–(2.3) of player $v$:
--   $$J_v = \tfrac12\,\mathbb E\Big[\int_0^T |\alpha_v(t,X(t))|^2\,dt + c\,\Big|X_v(T)-\frac{1}{\deg_G(v)}\sum_{u\sim v}X_u(T)\Big|^2\Big],$$
--   where for an isolated vertex the bracketed deviation is $X_v(T)$.
--   4. **Markovian $\varepsilon$-Nash equilibrium** (Definition 2.2), $\varepsilon=(\varepsilon_i)_{i=1}^n\in\mathbb R_+^n$: every $\alpha_i$ is admissible and for every player $i$ and every $\beta\in\mathcal A_G$,
--   $$J_i(\alpha)\le J_i(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_{i-1},\beta,\alpha_{i+1},\dots,\alpha_n)+\varepsilon_i .$$
--   A Nash equilibrium is the case $\varepsilon=0$.
--
--   This is the $n$-player linear–quadratic flocking game in which each player only cares about the average terminal position of her neighbours.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Vertices are `Fin n`. Each solution of (2.1) carries its own probability space and Brownian motion (`StateSol`); since (2.1) has a unique strong solution (the paper's reference [41]), all solutions have the same law, so comparing costs across solutions is the paper's notion. Costs are $[0,\infty]$-valued lower Lebesgue integrals. A Lean control is a function of $t\in\mathbb R$, jointly Borel on $\mathbb R\times\mathbb R^V$; its values outside $[0,T]$ are never used (neither by the state equation nor by the cost), and the linear-growth bound is required on $[0,T]$ only, so every control of the paper is represented, e.g. by its extension by $0$. The vector $\varepsilon$ is typed `Fin n → ℝ` and enters through `ENNReal.ofReal`, so a negative entry acts as $0$; the paper takes $\varepsilon\in\mathbb R_+^n$, and every statement using the notion has nonnegative $\varepsilon$.
-- source:
--   Lacker, Soret, A case study on stochastic games on large graphs in mean field and sparse regimes, arXiv:2005.14102v2 (2021), §2.1, pp. 4–5, (2.1)–(2.3), Definition 2.2

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_EthierKurtz_SDEState
import Definitions.Def_EthierKurtz_IsStandardBrownian
import Definitions.Def_EthierKurtz_completedBrownianPast

open MeasureTheory
open scoped ENNReal NNReal

namespace GraphLQGame.Equilibrium

open EthierKurtz

/-- Terminal deviation of player `v` from the average of its neighbours, `x_v − deg(v)⁻¹ Σ_{u∼v} x_u`
(the term inside the square of (2.2), p. 4); for an isolated vertex it is `x_v`, as in (2.3).
The terminal cost uses its square, so the order of subtraction is immaterial. -/
noncomputable def dev {n : ℕ} (G : SimpleGraph (Fin n)) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (v : Fin n)
    (x : SDEState n) : ℝ :=
  if G.degree v = 0 then x v
  else x v - ((G.degree v : ℝ))⁻¹ * ∑ u ∈ G.neighborFinset v, x u

/-- The admissible Markovian controls `𝒜_G` (§2.1, p. 4): Borel functions `α(t, x)` with
`sup_{(t,x) ∈ [0,T] × ℝ^V} |α(t, x)| / (1 + |x|) < ∞`.

Formalization Note: time is real; the linear-growth bound is required on `[0, T]` only, and values
at other times are never used. -/
def IsAdmissible {n : ℕ} (T : ℝ) (a : ℝ → SDEState n → ℝ) : Prop :=
  Measurable (Function.uncurry a) ∧
    ∃ C : ℝ, ∀ t ∈ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T, ∀ x : SDEState n, |a t x| ≤ C * (1 + ‖x‖)

/-- A (pathwise strong) solution of the state equation (2.1),
`dX_v(t) = α_v(t, X(t)) dt + σ dW_v(t)`, `t ∈ [0, T]`, `X(0) = x0` non-random, for the control
profile `a = (α_v)_v` (§2.1, p. 4).

The solution carries its own probability space and an `n`-dimensional standard Brownian motion
`W`; `X` has continuous paths on `[0, T]`, is adapted to the completed filtration generated by `W`
(so it is a strong solution, a functional of `W`), and satisfies, almost surely, for all
`t ∈ [0, T]` and all `i`,
`X_i(t) = x0_i + ∫₀ᵗ α_i(s, X(s)) ds + σ W_i(t)`. -/
structure StateSol (n : ℕ) (T σ : ℝ) (x0 : SDEState n) (a : Fin n → ℝ → SDEState n → ℝ) where
  /-- The sample space. -/
  Ω : Type
  [mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω]
  /-- The probability measure. -/
  P : Measure Ω
  [isProb : IsProbabilityMeasure P]
  /-- The driving `n`-dimensional standard Brownian motion `(W_v)_v`. -/
  W : ℝ≥0 → Ω → SDEState n
  hW : IsStandardBrownian P W
  /-- The state process. -/
  X : ℝ → Ω → SDEState n
  meas : ∀ t, Measurable (X t)
  cont : ∀ ω, ContinuousOn (fun t => X t ω) (Set.Icc 0 T)
  adapted : ∀ t ∈ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T,
    Measurable[completedBrownianPast P W (fun _ => x0) t.toNNReal] (X t)
  eqn : ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, ∀ t ∈ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T, ∀ i : Fin n,
    X t ω i = x0 i + (∫ s in (0 : ℝ)..t, a i s (X s ω)) + σ * W t.toNNReal ω i

attribute [instance] StateSol.mΩ StateSol.isProb

/-- The cost (2.2)–(2.3) of player `v` under a solution `S` of the state equation for the profile
`a`:
`J_v = ½ E[∫₀ᵀ |α_v(t, X(t))|² dt + c |X_v(T) − deg(v)⁻¹ Σ_{u∼v} X_u(T)|²]`
(for an isolated `v` the bracket is `X_v(T)`, as in (2.3)).

Formalization Note: the cost is an `ℝ≥0∞`-valued lower Lebesgue integral, so it never takes a junk
value for non-integrable integrands. -/
noncomputable def cost {n : ℕ} (G : SimpleGraph (Fin n)) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (c T : ℝ)
    {σ : ℝ} {x0 : SDEState n} {a : Fin n → ℝ → SDEState n → ℝ} (S : StateSol n T σ x0 a)
    (v : Fin n) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ∫⁻ ω, ENNReal.ofReal (1 / 2) *
      ((∫⁻ t in Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T, ENNReal.ofReal ((a v t (S.X t ω)) ^ 2)) +
        ENNReal.ofReal (c * (dev G v (S.X T ω)) ^ 2)) ∂S.P

/-- Markovian `ε`-Nash equilibrium (Definition 2.2, p. 5), `ε = (ε_i)_i`: every `α_i` is admissible,
and for every player `i` and every admissible deviation `β ∈ 𝒜_G`,
`J_i(α) ≤ J_i(α_1, …, α_{i−1}, β, α_{i+1}, …, α_n) + ε_i`.

Formalization Note: costs are compared across any solution `S` for `α` and any solution `S'` for
the deviated profile; by strong uniqueness for (2.1) (the paper's reference [41]) all solutions
have the same law, so this is the paper's notion. A Nash equilibrium is the case `ε = 0`. -/
def IsMarkovNash {n : ℕ} (G : SimpleGraph (Fin n)) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (c T σ : ℝ)
    (x0 : SDEState n) (ε : Fin n → ℝ) (a : Fin n → ℝ → SDEState n → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ i, IsAdmissible T (a i)) ∧
    ∀ (i : Fin n) (β : ℝ → SDEState n → ℝ), IsAdmissible T β →
      ∀ (S : StateSol n T σ x0 a) (S' : StateSol n T σ x0 (Function.update a i β)),
        cost G c T S i ≤ cost G c T S' i + ENNReal.ofReal (ε i)

end GraphLQGame.Equilibrium


